We're defining the NayaNET Living Interface System — the visual, interaction, motion, color, component, architectural, and experiential rules that every future AI or human engineer must follow.

And I want to make one important distinction before locking it:

“Everything is alive” does NOT mean everything constantly moves.

It means nothing important feels dead, inert, accidental, or unfinished.

Every element has purpose, depth, state, feedback, and a reason for existing.

That distinction is what lets us get to 10/10 instead of visual chaos.

The best current interface guidance reinforces exactly this: clarity, purpose, familiarity, feedback, craft, delight, and simplicity should work together; custom buttons should have an explicit press state; motion should communicate feedback/state/navigation rather than merely decorate.

So I'm going to establish this as the master standard.

