2. ☝️ PUSH-BUTTON SIMPLICITY — 12%

This is one of NayaNET's defining principles.

The user should feel:

See it → understand it → press it → something happens.

No hunting.

No tiny links.

No ambiguous icons.

No “Where do I go?”

No five-step workflow when one tap will do.

The rule:
If it can be one button, make it one button.

The button itself should communicate:

WHAT IT DOES

and

WHAT WILL HAPPEN NEXT.

10/10 means:

A user can accomplish the primary action with:

one obvious interaction.

Apple's current guidance similarly emphasizes recognizable buttons, sufficient hit regions, clear content, and an explicit press state.

3. ⚡ LIVING RESPONSIVENESS — 12%

This is where NayaNET becomes NayaNET.

Every interactive element has states.

Button:

REST → HOVER → FOCUS → PRESS → ACTIVE → SUCCESS

Orb:

REST → AWARE → APPROACH → TOUCH → OPEN → ACTIVE

Card:

REST → LIFT → PRESS → SELECT → TRANSFORM

Player:

IDLE → LOADING → READY → PLAYING → PAUSED → COMPLETE

Nothing simply jumps from:

“nothing”

to

“new screen.”

There is a relationship between the user's action and the interface response.

Apple's guidance explicitly notes that without a press state a custom button can feel unresponsive, and its responsiveness guidance emphasizes the perceived connection between input and resulting motion.

NayaNET enhancement:

We make that relationship beautiful.

